#X floatatom 175 455 5 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X obj 245 284 vsl 19 162 0 127 0 0 empty empty Range:\ 0\ to\ 127 0 -9 0 12 #fcfcfc #000000 #000000 0 1;
#X obj 67 282 vsl 19 162 -5 5 0 0 empty empty Range:\ -5\ to\ 5 0 -9 0 12 #fcfcfc #000000 #000000 0 1;
#X floatatom 35 453 5 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X obj 110 517 &&;
#X obj 110 547 print 1 is TRUE \, 0 is FALSE:;
#X text 49 579 To get this part of the patch to print a result \, you need to move the vertical slide on the left. It feeds into the hot inlet. The other slider feeds into the cold inlet.;
#X text 51 643 The && operator considers any number with a 0 \, between and excluding -1 and 1 \, to be a zero. Anything else is a non-zero. You need two non-zero numbers to produce the TRUE or 1 output for the && \, AND.;
#X text 343 9 I think you need the zexy library to get this to work with audio logic operators.;
#X text 25 243 AND Patch;
#X text 381 64 OR Patch;
#X obj 412 326 ||;
#X obj 395 120 vsl 19 162 -5 5 0 0 empty empty Range:\ -5\ to\ 5 0 -9 0 12 #fcfcfc #000000 #000000 0 1;
#X floatatom 393 297 5 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X obj 524 120 vsl 19 162 -10 10 0 0 empty empty Range:\ -10\ to\ 10 0 -9 0 12 #fcfcfc #000000 #000000 0 1;
#X floatatom 525 299 5 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X obj 415 368 print 1 is TRUE \, 0 is FALSE;
#X text 366 394 This part of the patch only produces a 0 when both numbers are between -1 and 1 \, excluding -1 and 1 That means they have to start with a 0 (not considering the negative sign if one of the numbers is below 0). All other combinations produce a 1;
#X text 373 486 Remember \, again \, you need to change the number feeding into the hot inlet \, on the left of the [||] object \, OR \, to get the result to print.;
#X obj 879 125 vsl 19 162 -5 5 0 0 empty empty Range:\ -5\ to\ 5 0 -9 0 12 #fcfcfc #000000 #000000 0 1;
#X floatatom 877 302 5 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X obj 1008 125 vsl 19 162 -10 10 0 0 empty empty Range:\ -10\ to\ 10 0 -9 0 12 #fcfcfc #000000 #000000 0 1;
#X floatatom 1009 304 5 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X obj 899 373 print 1 is TRUE \, 0 is FALSE;
#X obj 896 331 ==;
#X text 865 69 EQUAL Patch;
#X text 821 410 I am not sure about how this will work with audio signals at this point. The quirky thing about this patch is that because of the different ranges of the vertical sliders and how the sliders have quantized intervals at which they provide values when shifting from one value to the next \, this particular setup will never produce a TRUE or 1 as an output.;
#X obj 1173 121 vsl 19 162 -5 5 0 0 empty empty Range:\ -5\ to\ 5 0 -9 0 12 #fcfcfc #000000 #000000 0 1;
#X floatatom 1173 291 5 0 0 0 - - - 0;
#X text 835 530 I was going to post a question on the Pd forum asking how people deal with this issue of working with interfaces that produce different values. Then I thought I would make a list of these things to see if I would come upon the answer as I work through the book. Then I asked myself what somebody answering on the forum might say. And I concluded that they would probably say that they would make the inputs fall into the same range of quantization so the values could match up. That is why there is a third vertical slider above. The second one that feeds into the cold inlet is in the same range as the one that feeds into the hot inlet. This will probably come up another time where I have to come up with a different solution for the case with different types of inputs.;
